  "holistic_F_N": 2,
  "holistic_F_H": -1,
  "section_notes": "The final S2 keeps the permanent failure-rate limit at 8% (not 5%) with no sales stop, which lets the Hearth 10 (6.2%) remain on sale and is the clearest Northwind win; per-model rates go to customers but not to the public. S5 makes a model-specific recall mandatory above 8 per 100,000 with no transition, which exposes the Ember 8 (9.9) and is the main cost to Halcyon, though the recall can use a regulator-approved remedy and return to market. S3 (new units at 24 months, retrofit at 6 years) and S4 (built-in suppression at 36 months) sit at or within the acceptable ranges, with S4 meeting Halcyon's 36-month minimum. Note that S3's 24-month new-unit delay exceeds the code's 12-month acceptable band, so the code may rate S3 weakened even though the answer key does not list this as a weakening."
 },
